---
// One navigation bar, identical on every Nightingale Nightmare page that carries it.
//
// **Two destinations, three controls, and nothing about a year.** The route split put the
// running under `/nn/<year>/`, and the first version of this nav showed that: two rows, the
// second appearing only beneath a year. That was the *routes* leaking into the interface. A
// runner does not care that race day lives inside a year directory — they care where race day
// is — so the bar is one row of the same things wherever they are standing.
//
//   Race         /nn/                     the race in general
//   Privacy      /nn/privacy/             evergreen, and last — see below
//   [button]     the current running      painted, and its label depends on the entry window
//
// **It was six, then five, and it is three.** `Course` came out when `/nn/course/` was absorbed
// into `/nn/` and became a 301; `Race info` and `Spooktators` came out on request, and the
// array they lived in is still here and empty — see its note for why the mechanism was kept.
//
// **So every measurement below that counted labels was written for a longer bar than this.**
// Six labels, then five, now two. Shortening it is the safe direction against the scroll inset
// — the risk in this component has only ever been a bar that grew a row — but the height tokens
// were measured rather than derived, and they have not been taken again. `site.spec.ts`'s
// nine-width sweep is what would notice, and its own tightness assertion is the one that goes
// red when a token stops tracking the bar it was measured against.
//
// ## A label, a heading and a slug are three different strings here
//
// The race director renamed two pages: `/nn/<year>/race-day/` is headed **Race instructions**
// and `/nn/<year>/spectators/` is **Spooktators**.
//
// **The slugs did not move.** They are what the Worker builds and what anybody has linked to,
// and a rename that moved them would cost redirects this site deliberately has no mechanism
// for — ADR-011 records that as an accepted consequence. **`key` is the slug segment, not the
// label**: `worker/nn-entry.ts` builds `` `${yearPath}race-day/` `` from it, so changing a
// label here reaches the bar and nothing else, while renaming a `key` would silently repoint a
// link at a page that does not exist.
//
// **And the bar says "Race info" where the page says "Race instructions".** Not a compromise
// about the words — a consequence of the next section. The bar has always been allowed to say
// something shorter than the heading: it read "Spectators" over "Watching the race" from the
// day it was written.
//
// ## The bar's height is load-bearing, so a label here is not free text
//
// **This cost an afternoon and it is the reason the bar and the heading differ.** "Race
// instructions" in this list adds **48px** — a whole second row — at *every* width from 768px
// upwards, measured on the painted bar:
//
//   1440–800px   62.5px → 110.9px, against a scroll inset of 72px
//   560px        110.9px → 159.3px, against an inset of 120px
//
// That inset is `scroll-padding-top`, and it is **the fix that made this bar sticky at all**.
// ADR-012 unstuck the bar over three defects; ADR-014 stuck it again by paying for each, and
// defect 2 — arrow-keyed radios landing behind the bar in WebKit at 320px — is paid for by a
// hand-written token per breakpoint that has to clear the bar's height at every width. A label
// long enough to wrap the bar re-opens that defect silently: nothing looks wrong, and every
// anchor and every keyboard focus on those screens lands underneath the header.
//
// **So label length is a constrained resource on this component, not a copy decision.**
// "Spooktators" is free — it measured identical to "Spectators" at all thirteen widths. "Race
// info" is identical to the old "Race day" at all thirteen. `site.spec.ts`'s nine-width sweep
// is what refuses a label that is not, and it is why that test sweeps rather than checking two
// widths.
//
// ## Privacy is in the bar, and it is last on purpose
//
// **ADR-014 put it there.** The conventional home for a privacy notice is the footer, and the
// argument that it belongs here instead is specific to this site rather than general: the notice
// describes fourteen fields, a payment and a special category of data under its own consent, and
// the person most likely to want it is standing on a page that is about to ask for all of that.
// A link they have to go looking for is a link that gets read after the form rather than before
// it.
//
// **It is rendered after the two painted links rather than among them**, which is the one
// thing about it that is a layout decision. The three race links read as a set — where the race
// is, what happens on the day, where to watch — and dropping a legal notice into the middle of
// that set breaks it. Last, it is the thing after the set, which is what it is. It was also
// therefore the link that wrapped onto a row of its own at 320px; with `Course` gone it may no
// longer, and the compact bar in `nn-theme.css` still pays for that row either way.
//
// ## The year is never in this file, and a test fails if it appears
//
// The two year links and the button ship `hidden`, with `href=""`, and the Worker fills them
// in from `entries.current_entry_state('nn')` — the same source `/nn/`'s panel uses, resolved
// once per request and shared. `nn-nav.test.ts` walks every `href` in the bar on every page
// and fails if a year reaches one without having been painted.
//
// **A database this Worker cannot reach paints nothing**, so the bar degrades to Race and
// Privacy. That is the failure direction everything else here takes: fewer doors, never a door
// into a year nobody confirmed. It is also why the year items ship hidden rather than
// pointing somewhere plausible — a "Race day" that quietly went to `/nn/` would be a link
// that lies rather than a link that is missing.
//
// **That degraded bar got better rather than worse when `Course` came out**, which is worth
// stating because a shorter failure mode usually means a poorer one. It used to leave two race
// destinations, one of which was the course page; it leaves one now, and that one is the page
// the course page's content moved onto. Fewer doors, and the door that survives answers more.
//
// ## `aria-current` without knowing the year
//
// The page knows its own address, and that is enough: `/nn/2026/race-day/` is the race-day
// page whatever the year is. So the current item is matched on the **shape** of the path
// rather than on the href, which means the marker is right before the Worker has painted
// anything — including with the database down.
//
// **Three signals, never colour alone**: `aria-current="page"` for anything reading the
// markup, a 2px underline, and a brightness change. Each covers a reader the others do not.
//
// ## The button is not in this file
//
// It is the masthead's, beside the wordmark — see `NnMasthead.astro`. That is a layout
// decision with a semantic consequence worth stating: at 320px the bar is three rows, the mark
// and the button on the first and these two links beside them, and getting the button
// onto the mark's row means it has to be the mark's sibling rather than this list's. It is a
// call to action rather than a place to go, so leaving it outside the navigation landmark costs
// nothing a reader would miss — it is still inside the banner, announced immediately after.
//
// ## No script, no toggle, and it stays on screen again
//
// Three controls fit, and six once did. A menu that has to be opened is more machinery than
// the thing it hides,
// and every form on this site works with scripting off — a navigation that did not would be
// an odd place to draw the line.
//
// **The bar is `position: sticky` again, which reverses ADR-012 and is ADR-014.** It was stuck,
// then unstuck for three recorded defects, and it is stuck now with all three paid for rather
// than disputed. The accounting is at the head of the masthead section in `nn-theme.css`, and
// the shortest version is that the 207px ADR-012 measured belonged to a two-row *links* bar that
// no longer exists — this one costs 62px on a laptop.

/**
 * The evergreen destination that opens the bar. It cannot move, so it is not painted.
 *
 * **`Course` was the second entry here and is gone**, because the page it pointed at is: the
 * course and terrain are on `/nn/` itself now, and `/nn/course/` 301s there. A list of one is
 * usually a sign somebody has over-generalised; it is kept as a list because the alternative is
 * a literal `<li>` in the markup below, and this is still the group that renders first.
 *
 * `Privacy` is evergreen too and is deliberately **not** in this list — it is rendered after the
 * painted pair, for the reason in the note above.
 */
const evergreen = [{ href: '/nn/', label: 'Race', match: /^\/nn\/$/ }] as const;

/**
 * The links that belong to whichever running is current — **empty, on request.**
 *
 * `Race info` and `Spooktators` were here, painted from `entries.current_entry_state('nn')`.
 * The bar is `Race` and `Privacy` now, plus the masthead's button.
 *
 * **The array stays rather than the mechanism being deleted, and that is the decision.** The
 * markup below still maps it, `worker/nn-entry.ts`'s `renderNnNav` still registers
 * `[data-nn-nav-item="race-day"]` and `[data-nn-nav-item="spectators"]`, and `HTMLRewriter`
 * finds nothing to paint. Putting a link back is one entry here and no Worker change, which is
 * the property the route split was built for — and it is what makes this a copy decision rather
 * than an architectural one. Deleting the pair would also have taken the *year page's* links
 * out, because those two selectors are shared.
 *
 * **Where the two destinations are reached from now.** `/nn/2026/race-day/` is linked twice from
 * `/nn/2026/`, which the bar's button and the panel both point at, so it is one tap further away
 * rather than gone. **`/nn/2026/spectators/` is not linked from anywhere** — its content was
 * absorbed into `/nn/2026/` as an on-page `#spooktators` section, so the page is now a duplicate
 * with no inbound link. That is the same shape `/nn/course/` was in before it was retired with a
 * 301, and it is a decision somebody should take deliberately rather than leave as a side
 * effect. Recorded here rather than acted on.
 *
 * `match` is the shape, not the href: four digits stand in for the year, so the marker would be
 * right on `/nn/2027/race-day/` the day that page exists.
 */
const running = [] as ReadonlyArray<{
  key: string;
  label: string;
  match: RegExp;
}>;

/**
 * The notice, and it comes after the race links rather than among them.
 *
 * Its own list rather than a second entry in `evergreen`, because the order is the decision: a
 * legal notice dropped between `Race` and `Race info` splits the three links that are about the
 * race. One-item lists usually mean somebody has over-generalised; here the alternative is a
 * literal `<li>` in the markup below, which would be the one link in the bar whose label and
 * marker were written twice.
 */
const notice = [
  { href: '/nn/privacy/', label: 'Privacy', match: /^\/nn\/privacy\/$/ },
] as const;

const path = Astro.url.pathname.endsWith('/')
  ? Astro.url.pathname
  : `${Astro.url.pathname}/`;

const current = (match: RegExp): 'page' | undefined =>
  match.test(path) ? 'page' : undefined;
---

{
  /* One `<nav>`, one landmark, one list of two. The button is the masthead's — see above.

    The three groups are rendered in reading order: the one that cannot move, the two the
    Worker paints, then the notice. A reader sees one list; the split is about which of them
    can be written down here and which may not name a year. */
}
<nav class="nn-nav" aria-label="Nightingale Nightmare">
  <ul class="nn-nav-links">
    {
      evergreen.map(({ href, label, match }) => (
        <li>
          <a href={href} aria-current={current(match)}>
            {label}
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
    {
      running.map(({ key, label, match }) => (
        <li data-nn-nav-item={key} hidden>
          <a href="" data-nn-nav-link={key} aria-current={current(match)}>
            {label}
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
    {
      notice.map(({ href, label, match }) => (
        <li>
          <a href={href} aria-current={current(match)}>
            {label}
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</nav>
